# conflict-waker.conf.example — every value the conflict waker installer reads.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOW TO USE THIS FILE
#
#   cp conflict-waker.conf.example conflict-waker.conf
#   $EDITOR conflict-waker.conf      # one value you must set: REPO_DIRS
#   python3 scripts/pipeline_conflict_waker_setup.py run
#
# On a machine running Stage E, put it beside stage-e.conf and the Stage E
# installer's `conflict-waker` step runs the same steps for you.
#
# WHAT IT INSTALLS.  A LaunchAgent for YOU, the person whose local Claude Code
# sessions it wakes.  When one of their pull requests goes CONFLICTING, the
# conflict monitor posts a fix request; each pass of the waker claims requests
# for branches whose worktree one of YOUR sessions worked in, and starts a
# capped fix session there.  A dispatcher's pull requests are never its
# business — the bounce driver sends those back to the session's own thread.
#
# FORMAT.  `KEY=value`, one per line; `#` starts a comment only at the start of
# a line.  Parsed, never sourced.  A duplicate key is an error.  No value here
# ever holds a credential — the waker uses your own `claude` and `gh` logins.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The checkouts whose worktrees your sessions run in — the directory you start
# parallel sessions from, whose `git worktree list` holds their worktrees.
# RULE: absolute paths, comma-separated, each a checkout ROOT with a GitHub
# origin.  The value below is refused as typed, so an unedited file cannot run.
REPO_DIRS=/Users/you/src/your-project

# The LaunchAgent's label.  RULE: reverse-DNS.
LABEL=local.pr-conflict-waker

# Where the waker's own code is cloned from.  Empty means the origin of the
# first REPO_DIRS entry, which must carry scripts/pr_conflict.py on its default
# branch.  The job never execs code from a checkout your sessions work in.
CODE_REPO_URL=

# Seconds between passes.  launchd starts each one; there is no loop.
INTERVAL_SECONDS=300

# The bounds.  Per fix session: a spend cap in US dollars and a timeout in
# minutes.  Per pass: a session cap.  MAX_SESSIONS_PER_PASS x TIMEOUT_MIN must
# fit inside the conflict monitor's two-hour result deadline, less ten minutes,
# or the installer refuses the conf — a queued session must never still be
# working when the monitor pages about it.  Per pull request, lifetime, the
# monitor allows three requests; that bound is not configurable here.
MAX_BUDGET_USD=5
TIMEOUT_MIN=40
MAX_SESSIONS_PER_PASS=2

# How the fix session starts: `from-pr` resumes the session linked to the pull
# request where Claude Code has one; `continue` resumes the worktree's latest;
# `fresh` starts a new one.
RESUME_MODE=from-pr

# Extra arguments for `claude`, space-separated.  A headless session gets only
# the tools your settings and these allow; one that cannot run git and gh ends
# `failed` and the monitor pages you.  Match how you run your own sessions, for
# example:  --permission-mode acceptEdits --allowedTools Bash(git:*) Bash(gh:*)
CLAUDE_ARGS=
